text:
Checking the following code, could you confirm that is does not work concurrently ?
The &quot;world&quot; var is read-only, does it mean that all other entities are also readonly (cannot be modified by another thread, for instance) ?<p><pre><code>  struct World {
    entities Vec&lt;Entity&gt;;
  }
  func step(world &amp;World, entity in world.entities[] mut) {
    entity.advance();
    let collision = world.get_collision_for_entity(entity);
    entity.resolve(collision);
  }</code></pre>

I was wrong. The bulk of my notifications are from Austin Energy. But ERCOT does still send them too. This is my last one:<p>&gt; Austin Energy: ERCOT expects tight grid conditions, requests conservation Tue., Jan. 16, 6-9 am. Similar conditions expected Wed. morning. More info: ercot.com&#x2F;txans . Conservation tips: austinenergy.com&#x2F;alerts . 
&gt;
&gt; ERCOT espera condiciones de red ajustadas, solicita conservación Martes, 16 de enero, 6-9 am. Se esperan condiciones similares el miércoles mañana. Más información: ercot.com&#x2F;txans . Consejos de conservación: austinenergy.com&#x2F;alerts . Responde STOP para cancelar. Reply STOP to cancel.<p>&gt; it&#x27;s been addressed. We had one of our hottest Augusts on record this year without a single issue.<p>No significant changes to weathercation regulation. No significant changes to the energy market. What exactly, from a systemic perspective, do you feel &quot;has been addressed&quot;? What little I see is too little.<p>&gt; If you think it&#x27;s structurally broken there are energies futures markets you could use to make a lot of money on.<p>What on earth? My problem is that the market is built to incentivize profit over preventing human pain and suffering. That&#x27;s not a &quot;make money here&quot; opportunity.

I understand what you&#x27;re saying, yet I&#x27;ve tried running on Linux<p><pre><code>    env LANG=en_US.ISO-8859-1 python3.8 -c &quot;import locale; print(locale.getpreferredencoding(False)); print(&#x27;€urope&#x27;); print(&#x27;STOP&#x27;)&quot;
</code></pre>
and it worked fine.
